<Styles xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        xmlns:luna="clr-namespace:EmuSen.LunaP.Controls">

    <!-- A header row above a list, sharing its column widths with every row in
         it - see docs/LunaP.md §27.

         Grid.IsSharedSizeScope is what makes an Auto column line up: the header
         grid and each row grid are separate grids, so without a shared scope an
         Auto column would size itself independently in every one of them and
         nothing would align. The scope goes on the root so both parts are
         inside it.

         This half is necessary and not sufficient, and the other half is not
         here. A definition only actually joins the scope if it is ADDED to a
         grid's own collection rather than assigned as a ready-made one - see
         LunaTable.Define, and docs/LunaP.md §27.7. Setting the scope here and
         getting that wrong leaves a control that looks correct everywhere
         except on screen, which is how it shipped.

         The header's padding matches the item container's below, because those
         two numbers are what actually decide whether a column heading sits over
         its own cells.

         `:is(...)` AND NOT A BARE TYPE SELECTOR, which is the whole reason the
         first draft of this style did nothing at all. An Avalonia type selector
         is an EXACT match: `luna|LunaTable` selects instances whose type is
         precisely LunaTable, and every instance there will ever be is a
         LunaTable<T> deriving from it. The symptom is the one §5.5 keeps
         recording - a control with no template, no error, and nothing on
         screen. Every other style in the kit names a type that is instantiated
         directly, which is why the trap has not appeared elsewhere. -->
    <Style Selector=":is(luna|LunaTable)">
        <Setter Property="Grid.IsSharedSizeScope" Value="True" />
        <Setter Property="Template">
            <ControlTemplate>
                <DockPanel>
                    <!-- ClipToBounds, because the header is what SCROLLS SIDEWAYS and a
                         translated grid paints outside its parent unless something
                         says otherwise. Without it a table scrolled right draws its
                         leftmost heading over the window beside it. §59. -->
                    <Border DockPanel.Dock="Top"
                            BorderBrush="{DynamicResource LunaBorder}"
                            BorderThickness="0,0,0,1"
                            ClipToBounds="True"
                            Padding="12,4,12,6">
                        <Grid Name="PART_Header" />
                    </Border>
                    <!-- WHY THE VALIDATION MESSAGE IS HERE AND NOT IN THE CELL.
                         A cell is a few characters wide and already full of the
                         editor; a message inside one would either be clipped or
                         would widen the column while somebody is typing in it.
                         Docked under the whole table it has room for a sentence,
                         and it is the same "message below the thing it is about"
                         FieldRow settled on (§49.4).

                         Collapsed rather than reserved: a table that keeps an
                         empty line under it for a message it usually has nothing
                         to say is a table that is shorter than it looks. §50. -->
                    <luna:ErrorText Name="PART_Error"
                                    DockPanel.Dock="Bottom"
                                    Margin="12,4"
                                    IsVisible="False" />

                    <!-- HORIZONTAL SCROLLING, AND WHY IT IS ONE ATTACHED PROPERTY RATHER
                         THAN A SCROLLBAR OF THIS CONTROL'S OWN. A ListBox already
                         contains a ScrollViewer; Fluent leaves its horizontal bar
                         Disabled, which is what made columns past the right edge
                         unreachable by scrollbar, wheel or keyboard - measured, not
                         inferred: six columns of 200 in a 400-wide table resolved all
                         six definitions to 200 and clipped the grid at 376, with the
                         viewer reporting extent equal to viewport (§59).

                         Turning it on gets the bar, the wheel, the keyboard and touch
                         from the ScrollViewer that was already there. The header is the
                         only part outside it, and LunaTable translates that to match -
                         which is the whole of the code this needs. Auto rather than
                         Visible, so a table whose columns fit shows no bar and loses no
                         height. -->
                    <ListBox Name="PART_Rows"
                             Background="Transparent"
                             BorderThickness="0"
                             ScrollViewer.HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Auto" />
                </DockPanel>
            </ControlTemplate>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!-- A SORTABLE HEADING IS A BUTTON WEARING NOTHING. LunaTable.Heading makes
         one for any column with a comparison, because a heading that answers
         only a click is a sort a keyboard user does not have (§24) - a Button
         brings focus, Tab, Space, Enter and an invoke peer for free.

         What it also brings is Fluent's chrome, and all of this is taking it
         off again: the heading has to sit on the same baseline and the same x
         as the plain TextBlock in the column beside it, or a table with one
         sortable column among three has a heading row that steps up and down.

         Background is set on the ContentPresenter and not on the Button. Fluent
         paints a Button's fill in its template, so a Setter on the control is
         overridden by the theme the moment a pointer enters; the pointerover and
         pressed rules below reach the presenter, which is what actually draws. -->
    <Style Selector=":is(luna|LunaTable) Button.heading">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="0" />
        <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="0" />
        <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="0" />
        <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Hand" />
        <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Stretch" />
        <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Left" />
    </Style>

    <Style Selector=":is(luna|LunaTable) Button.heading /template/ ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="0" />
    </Style>

    <!-- The hover affordance, and the only one: a heading that looks identical
         whether it sorts or not gives a mouse user nothing to discover. The
         glyph tells them afterwards; this is what tells them before. -->
    <Style Selector=":is(luna|LunaTable) Button.heading:pointerover /template/ ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource LunaInputSurface}" />
    </Style>

    <Style Selector=":is(luna|LunaTable) Button.heading:pressed /template/ ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource LunaInputSurface}" />
    </Style>

    <!-- The sort direction. Hidden entirely in the third state rather than shown
         as a neutral mark, so the cycle reads as two sorted states and off. -->
    <Style Selector=":is(luna|LunaTable) TextBlock.sort">
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource LunaInfo}" />
    </Style>

    <!-- THE GUTTER DOWN THE LEFT, and the corner above it. LunaMuted rather than LunaText, because a
         row header is how you REFER to a row rather than something the row says - the same relation a
         line number has to a line of code, and every editor draws it quieter than the code. It is
         held to 4.22:1 dark and 5.75:1 light against the surface (measured for §57.3), so quieter
         does not mean unreadable.

         Right-aligned, which matters for the case this is mostly for: numbers and hex addresses read
         down a column by their last digit, and a left-aligned gutter of 9, 10, 11 puts the units
         under the tens. §58. -->
    <Style Selector=":is(luna|LunaTable) TextBlock.row-header">
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource LunaMuted}" />
        <Setter Property="TextAlignment" Value="Right" />
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,0,8,0" />
    </Style>

    <!-- THE GRID LINES, off by default and drawn in LunaBorder when on. That token already means
         "where one surface stops and the next begins" (§26.9) and is already held to 3:1 against
         both surfaces, which is exactly what a rule between cells is - so it takes the token rather
         than introducing a colour of its own. §56. -->
    <Style Selector=":is(luna|LunaTable) Border.row-rule">
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource LunaBorder}" />
    </Style>

    <Style Selector=":is(luna|LunaTable) Border.column-rule">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource LunaBorder}" />
    </Style>

    <!-- WHERE THE FROZEN COLUMNS STOP. Takes LunaBorder for the same reason the
         grid rules do (§56.2): it is where one surface stops and the next
         begins, and that token is already held to 3:1 against both surfaces.
         Drawn whether or not the table has been scrolled yet, because a layout
         that behaves differently on the left should say so before the user
         discovers it rather than after. §63.

         Its own class rather than reusing column-rule, so a host that wants the
         boundary louder than a grid line - a heavier stroke, the accent - can
         restyle one without the other. -->
    <Style Selector=":is(luna|LunaTable) Border.frozen-edge">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource LunaBorder}" />
    </Style>

    <!-- The other half of the alignment. Fluent's ListBoxItem padding is not the
         header's, so left alone the columns start at two different x positions;
         this pins both to 12. The vertical figure is smaller than Fluent's
         default because a table is read as rows of data rather than as a menu
         of choices, and Fluent's spacing puts four fields on a screen. -->
    <Style Selector=":is(luna|LunaTable) ListBoxItem">
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="12,4" />
        <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="0" />
    </Style>

</Styles>
